# Surface coatings of two-dimensional metal-organic framework nanosheets enable stable zinc anodes

1State Key Laboratory of Coordination Chemistry, School of Chemistry and Chemical Engineering, Nanjing University, Nanjing 210093, China; 2National and Local Joint Engineering Research, Center of Biomedical Functional Materials, School of Chemistry and Materials Science, Nanjing Normal University, Nanjing 210023, China; 3Jiangsu Engineering Research Center of Interfacial Chemistry, Nanjing University, Nanjing 210093, China
Aqueous zinc-ion batteries (ZIBs) have been considered as safe and scalable energy storage solutions, but the dendrite and corrosion issues of $Z\mathrm{n}$ anodes have hindered their further application. Herein, we demonstrate that two-dimensional metalorganic framework (MOF) nanosheets can act as protective coatings to prevent dendrite formation and hydrogen evolution of Zn anodes. The morphology of MOFs was tuned from octahedral nanoparticles (UiO-67-3D) to nanosheets (UiO-67-2D), leading to significantly enhanced protective performance. UiO-67-2D nanosheets-coated $Z\mathrm{n}$ anodes displayed smaller polarization, longer cycling lifetime and lower $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution than those of UiO-67-3D nanoparticles in symmetrical cells, which has been attributed to the higher concentration of surface $\mathrm{\Zr-OH/H_{2}O}$ to induce uniform $Z\mathrm{n}$ deposition and one-dimensional (1D) channels perpendicular to the $Z\mathrm{n}$ surface to regulate $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ diffusion. The assembled UiO- $67{-}2\mathrm{D}@\mathrm{Zn}||\mathrm{Mn}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{C}$ full cell shows a high capacity of $240\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ at $\mathrm{~1~A~g~}^{-1}$ and excellent cycling stability.
## metal-organic frameworks, nanosheets, zinc-ion battery, zinc anode
Citation: Lei L, Chen F, Wu Y, Shen J, Wu XJ, Wu S, Yuan S. Surface coatings of two-dimensional metal-organic framework nanosheets enable stable zinc anodes. Sc China Chem, 2022, 65: 2205–2213, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11426-022-1324-0
## 1 Introduction
Aqueous zinc-ion batteries (ZIBs) are promising candidates for large-scale energy storage systems owing to their high specific capacity (theoretical weight capacity of $820\mathrm{mAhg}^{-1}$ and volume capacity of $5,855\mathrm{mAhcm}^{-3}$ ), low cost, inherent safety, and environmental benignity of $Z\mathrm{n}$ [1–4]. However, the application of ZIBs has been seriously hindered by dendrite growth (resulting from nonuniform Zn deposition) [5], hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) (caused by thermodynamic instability of $Z\mathrm{n}$ in aqueous electrolytes) [6] and corrosion (self-corrosion and electrochemical corrosion) [7].
To address these challenges, several strategies have been proposed through the optimization of electrolytes [8], design of porous Zn anodes [9,10], and surface modification by protective coatings [11]. Among these strategies, surface modification by protective coatings has been shown to enhance the cycling stability of Zn anode by regulating the $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ diffusion, creating uniform nucleation sites, and isolating the Zn surface from corrosive aqueous electrolytes [12–14]. Various organic and inorganic materials have been attempted as protective layers of $Z\mathrm{n}$ anodes, such as organic polymers [15], carbon materials [16], metal oxides [17–19], and metal sulfides [20]. For example, modification of $Z\mathrm{n}$ surface with carbon has been shown to facilitate homogeneous current distribution and uniform Zn deposition [21]. However, the conductive nature of carbon coating promotes the Zn deposition and dendrite formation on the carbon surface, posing a threat to long-term cycling. Non-conductive layers, such as $\boldsymbol{Z}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{rO}}_{2}$ [17], $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ [18], $\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ [19], $\mathrm{HfO}_{2}$ [22], and $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ [23], which show significant inhibitory effects on dendrite formation and adverse reactions, have been explored. Nevertheless, the electrochemically inert layers may hinder the ion/electron transport and reduce the rate performance of protected $Z\mathrm{n}$ anodes. Therefore, electrically insulating and ionically conductive materials are highly desired as protective materials for $Z\mathrm{n}$ anodes, and metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are suitable candidates.
MOFs are porous crystalline materials constructed from inorganic metal nodes and organic linkers [24]. Compared with other organic and inorganic materials, such as organic polymers and metal oxides, MOFs exhibit the following merits in stabilizing $Z\mathrm{n}$ metal anodes. Firstly, the intrinsic pores of MOFs allow facile diffusion of $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ , while their insulating nature ensures the deposition of $Z\mathrm{n}$ under the MOF-protective layers. Secondly, the surface structure of MOFs can be judiciously tuned to regulate the interaction between Zn surface and electrolytes. The precisely defined crystal structure of MOFs further facilitates the understanding of the structure-property relationship to guide the rational design of Zn coatings. Finally, the morphology of MOFs can be controlled to form stable two-dimensional films attaching to the Zn surface. By engineering the surface of MOFs, we expect to realize uniform Zn nucleation and retard side reactions. Despite the aforementioned merits, only a handful of MOFs have been explored as protective coatings of $Z\mathrm{n}$ anodes, including ZIF-8 [25–29], HKUST-1 [30], UiO-66 [31], ZIF-7 [32], and Zn-BTC [33]. Although these works highlight the promise of MOFs as Zn anode coatings, these MOFs are usually obtained as nanoparticles and deposited onto $Z\mathrm{n}$ anodes without structural or morphological design.
Herein, we synthesized two MOFs, namely UiO-67-3D and UiO-67-2D, and studied the effect of structures and morphologies on their performance as Zn anode coatings. UiO-67-3D has a three-dimensional (3D) framework structure forming octahedral nanoparticles, whereas UiO-67-2D is a two-dimensional (2D) network with a nanosheet morphology. Coating UiO-67-2D layers on the $Z\mathrm{n}$ anode dramatically reduced the polarization (overpotential of $50\mathrm{mV}$ at $3\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ and elongated the cycling lifetime ( $850\mathrm{h}$ at 0.5 $\mathrm{mAh~cm}^{-2})$ , while the overpotential of UiO $)-67-3\mathrm{D}\mathscr{@}\mathrm{Zn}$ anode increased rapidly within $210\mathrm{h}$ under the same cycling condition. Furthermore, in situ gas chromatography-mass spectrometry (GC-MS) studies revealed that UiO-67-2D nanosheets-coated $Z\mathrm{n}$ anodes displayed lower hydrogen evolution $(<0.5\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{h}^{-1}\mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ than UiO-67-3D $(1.1~\mathrm{mmol}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ and bare $Z\mathrm{n}$ $(2.6~\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{h}^{-1}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ in symmetrical cells, which is in agreement with the coulombic efficiency (CE) measurements. The superior protective performance of UiO-67-2D compared with UiO-67-3D was attributed to the higher concentration of surface $\mathrm{\Zr{-}O H/H_{2}O}$ groups as zincophilic sites to allow uniform $Z\mathrm{n}$ nucleation, which was confirmed by density functional theory (DFT) calculations, diffuse reflectance infrared Fourier transform spectroscopy (DRIFTS), thermogravimetric analysis (TGA), and contact angle measurements. Besides, the parallel orientation of UiO-67-2D nanosheets on the $Z\mathrm{n}$ surface vertically aligns the MOF channels, which regulates $Z\mathrm{n}$ diffusion, leading to dendrite-free Zn deposition. The good performance of $\mathrm{JiO-}67{-}2\mathrm{D}@Z\mathrm{n}$ anode was further verified in a $\mathrm{UiO-67-2D@2n||Mn_{2}O_{3}/C}$ cell showing high capacity $(240\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at ${\mathfrak{l}}\operatorname{Ag}^{-1};$ and excellent cycling stability $\sim100\%$ CE and $81\%$ capacity retention after 1,500 cycles at $2\mathrm{Ag}^{-1})$ , which is much better than those of UiO-67- $3\mathrm{D}@\mathrm{Zn}||\mathrm{Mn}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{C}$ and bare $\mathrm{Zn}||\mathbf{Mn}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{3}/\mathrm{C}$ . This work highlighted the impact of MOF structures/morphologies on the $Z\mathrm{n}$ anode performance to guide the rational design of protective coatings for stable $Z\mathrm{n}$ anodes.
## 2 Results and discussion
## 2.1 Synthesis of UiO-67-3D nanoparticles and UiO-67- 2D nanosheets
To overcome the challenges of Zn anodes in dendrite formation and parasitic HER (Figure 1a), two MOFs with the same metal and ligand but different morphologies were selected. UiO-67-3D [34] formed octahedral nanoparticles, similar to previously reported MOF materials used as protective coatings of $Z\mathrm{n}$ anodes (Figure 1b inset) [25–33]. On the other hand, taking advantage of the high tunability of MOFs, 2D nanosheets were obtained, namely UiO-67-2D (Figure 1c inset) [35,36]. We propose that UiO-67-2D nanosheets may form denser layers than UiO-67-3D nanoparticles to regulate the diffusion of $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ (Figure 1b, c). Meanwhile, the exposed $\mathrm{Zr-OH/H_{2}O}$ on the surface of UiO67-2D nanosheets may provide nucleation sites for uniform Zn deposition.
By judiciously controlling the ${Z\mathrm{r}}^{4+}$ /BPDC $(\mathrm{{BPDC}=\ b i-}$ phenyl- $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -dicarboxylate) ratio and reaction temperature during the MOF-synthesis, UiO-67-3D and UiO-67-2D were successfully isolated. The structures of UiO-67-3D and UiO67-2D were confirmed by power X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns, which matched well with the simulations based on the reported crystal structures (Figure 2b) [34–36]. UiO-67- 3D is formed by connecting $\mathrm{\small{[Zr_{6}(\mu_{3}-O)_{4}(\mu_{3}-O H)_{4}](C O_{2})_{12}}]}$ clusters with 12 linear BPDC linkers into a 3D framework (Figure 2a, Figure S1a, Supporting Information online). On the other hand, UiO-67-2D has 2D network structures with $[Z\mathrm{r}_{12}(\upmu_{3}{-}\mathrm{O})_{8}(\upmu_{3}{-}\mathrm{OH})_{8}(\upmu_{2}{-}\mathrm{OH})_{6}](\mathrm{OH})_{6}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{6}(\mathrm{CO}_{2})_{12}$ cluster (Figure 2a, Figure S1b). Each $\boldsymbol{Z}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{r}}_{12}$ -cluster is coordinated to
12 carboxylate groups from BPDC linkers within the equatorial position, and terminated by six pairs of - ${\cdot}\mathrm{OH}/\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ on the top and bottom. UiO-67-3D and UiO-67-2D exhibit different porosities, as confirmed by the $\Nu_{2}$ sorption isotherms at $77\mathrm{K}$ (Figure 2c). Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface area of UiO-67-3D and UiO-67-2D was calculated to be 2,450 and $424\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , matching the literatures [34–36]. Pore size distributions derived from ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms reveal the pore sizes of 1.2 and $0.8\mathrm{nm}$ for UiO-67-3D and UiO-67-2D (Figure 2c inset), respectively, corresponding to the octahedral cages and triangular channels in the crystal structures of UiO-67-3D and UiO-67-2D (Figure 1). These results confirmed the successful synthesis of UiO-67-3D and UiO-67-2D.
The morphologies of UiO-67-3D and UiO-67-2D were characterized by scanning electron microscopy (SEM). UiO67-3D forms uniform octahedral nanoparticles with an average size of ${\sim}300\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 2d). In contrast, UiO-67-2D are nanoplates with an average edge size of ${\sim}200\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 2e). The orientation of UiO-67-2D was further revealed by high-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM). The hexagonal lattices can be clearly observed along the $c$ -axis (Figure $2\mathrm{f},\mathrm{g})$ ), indicating that the UiO-67-2D nanosheets were grown within the ab-plane perpendicular to the $c$ -axis. The triangular channels are aligned perpendicular to the nanosheets. The lattice fringe in HR-TEM images matchs well with the crystal structures of UiO-67-2D (Figure $2\mathrm{g}$ inset). This structural model agrees with the PXRD patterns of UiO-67-2D, in which the diffraction peaks of (00l) families were absent due to the low dimension along the $c$ - axis.
## 2.2 Fabrication of UiO-67-3D- and UiO-67-2D-coated Zn anodes
UiO-67-3D- and UiO-67-2D-coated $Z\mathrm{n}$ anodes were prepared by drop-casting a suspension of MOF particles onto a polished Zn foil. UiO-67-3D and UiO-67-2D remain stable and crystalline after depositing onto Zn anodes, as confirmed by their characteristic diffraction peaks in PXRD (Figure 3a). SEM image of UiO-67-3D@Zn shows loosely packed UiO67-3D nanoparticles on the surface without preferred orientations (Figure 3b). In contrast, UiO-67-2D nanosheets are parallelly stacked forming a denser protective layer (Figure 3c). Cross-section SEM images indicate the similar thickness $(\sim20\upmu\mathrm{m})$ of UiO-67-3D and UiO-67-2D layers on the $Z\mathrm{n}$ surface (Figure 3d, f). The position of UiO-67-3D and UiO-67-2D layers on the $Z\mathrm{n}$ foil was further confirmed by elemental mapping images with energy dispersive X-ray spectrometry (EDS). A clear layering of Zn and $Z\mathrm{r}$ elements was observed, in which $Z\mathrm{r}$ mainly existed in the MOF-based surface coating layer (Figure 3e, g) on the top of the Zn foil.
The porous MOF coatings exhibit poor electronic conductivity, but high ionic conductivity and high $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ transference number, enabling them as sturdy artificial solid electrolyte interphase layers. The ionic conductivity was calculated from electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) using symmetrical cells (Figure S2). The slightly higher ionic conductivity of UiO-67-2D $(2.65{\times}10^{-3}\mathrm{S}\mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ than UiO-67-3D $(1.13{\times}\mathrm{\bar{10}}^{-3}\mathrm{S}\mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ can be attributed to its 1D open channels aligned perpendicular to the $Z\mathrm{n}$ surface. On the other hand, the $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ transference number of the UiO67-2D reaches 0.84, which is higher than that of bare $Z\mathrm{n}$ (0.31) and UiO-67-3D (0.65, Figure S3), promising for more effective $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ conduction. The enhanced $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ transference number of UiO-67-2D can be related to its small and welloriented channels which allow fast $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ transportation while block the $\mathrm{SO_{4}}^{2-}$ anions. The selective binding of $\mathrm{SO_{4}}^{2-}$ anions by $\mathrm{Zr-OH/H_{2}O}$ of MOFs further inhibits the transport of $\mathrm{SO_{4}}^{2-}$ anions as demonstrated previously [37].
To study the effect of different MOF coatings on the $Z\mathrm{n}$ stripping/plating behavior, $\mathsf{M O F s}@Z\mathrm{n}$ electrodes symmetric cells were assembled where the rate performance of Zn, UiO-67-3D@Zn, and UiO-67-2D@Zn electrodes was measured at various current densities (Figure 4a). The UiO-67- $2\mathrm{D}@Z\mathrm{n}$ electrode displays low overpotential and stable voltage profiles. With the current density increasing from 0.25 to $3\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , the $Z\mathrm{n}$ symmetric cell with UiO-67-
$2\ensuremath{\mathrm{D}}\ensuremath{\circledast}\ensuremath{\mathrm{Zn}}$ exhibits a steadily increasing overpotential from 18 to $49~\mathrm{mV}$ (Figure 4b). Impressively, the overpotential of UiO-67-2D $@Z{\bmod{n}}$ symmetric cells is better than those of most Zn anodes with various coatings, highlighting the distinct advantage of 2D-MOF coatings (Figure 4c) [18,38–41]. For comparison, UiO- $67-3\mathrm{D}\mathscr{Q}\mathrm{Zn}$ symmetric cell exhibits voltage oscillations, while bare Zn-based cell has an abrupt rise in voltage at $3\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ as a result of dendrite-caused short circuit (Figure 4a). The reduced overpotential of MOFcoated Zn anodes is attributed to the enhanced $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ conductivity and reduced Zn nucleation barrier. First, the porous
MOF coatings exhibit poor electronic conductivity, but good $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ conductivity (Figure S2). The uniform $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ diffusion channels of porous materials will regulate the diffusion of $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ and reduce the mass transfer overpotential [26]. Second, the $\mathrm{\DeltaZr{\mathrm{-OH/H_{2}O}}}$ sites on the surface of MOF particles act as zincophilic sites to effectively reduce the $Z\mathrm{n}$ nucleation potential, which will be discussed in later section. The longterm galvanostatic cycling performance of $Z\mathrm{n}$ , UiO-67- $3\mathrm{D}@Z\mathrm{n}$ , and UiO-67-2D $@z\mathrm{n}$ electrodes was further evaluated using symmetrical cells at $0.5\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ (Figure 4d). Similar to the trend of rate performance, Ui $)-67-2\mathrm{D}\mathscr{@}Z\mathrm{n}$ shows the highest stability and lowest overpotentials among the three electrodes, and operated for $850\mathrm{h}$ without obvious changes in overpotentials (Figure 4d inset). In contrast, UiO67-3D-based Zn symmetric cells exhibit erratic voltage response with the gradually enlarged overpotential starting from $200\mathrm{h}$ , which is tentatively attributed to the protrusion of $Z\mathrm{n}$ dendrites through the loose UiO-67-3D layers. Bare Zn-based symmetric cells short-circuited after 71 h operation due to the dendrite puncturing through the separator. Furthermore, UiO $-67-2\mathrm{D}\textcircled{a}Z\mathrm{n}$ symmetric cell maintained fairly good stability at higher charging/discharging rate and capacity (150 h at $2\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , $2\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ (Figure S4a) and $60\mathrm{h}$ at $5\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , 5 mAh $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ (Figure S4b)), which is much higher than the UiO- $67-3\mathrm{D}\mathscr{Q}\mathrm{Zn}$ and bare Zn symmetric cells. This was supported by the SEM and elemental mapping images. Cross-section SEM images after rate tests $(0.25,0.5,1,2,3,0.25\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ for rate tests, each current density with $20\mathrm{h}$ cycle time) show the protrusion of $Z\mathrm{n}$ dendrites through the loose UiO-67-3D layers, whereas the dense UiO-67-2D layers remain intact. Elemental mappings further revealed the overlay of $Z\mathrm{n}$ and $Z\mathrm{r}$ elements within the UiO-67-3D layers, indicating the growth of Zn dendrites within the UiO-67-3D coatings (Figure S5a). In contrast, the Zn and $Z\mathrm{r}$ elements are well-separated on the UiO-67-2D/Zn interface, indicating the stability of UiO-67-2D coating to prevent $Z\mathrm{n}$ dendrites (Figure S5b). After rate tests (0.25, 0.5, $1,2,3,0.25\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ for rate tests, each current density with $20\mathrm{h}$ cycle time), the UiO-67-3D and UiO-67-2D coatings were further peeled off to show the morphology of the Zn surface. Unlike the dendrites and protuberance observed on bare $Z\mathrm{n}$ (Figure 5a) and UiO-67-3D@Zn (Figure 5b), the surface of UiO-67-2D-coated Zn remained smooth (Figure 5c). Therefore, the enhanced cycling stability of Zn anodes by MOF coatings can be attributed to the inhibition of dendrite growth. We further used PXRD to study the crystal orientation of deposited Zn on bare Zn, UiO-67-3D@Zn, and UiO-67-2D@Zn electrodes. Compared with the pristine Zn foil, the relative intensity of (002) peaks increased after deposition on bare Zn, UiO-67-3D@Zn, and UiO-67-2D $@Z{\bmod{n}}$ electrodes, indicating the preferred growing along (002) direction (Figure S6). This is consistent with the growth of dendrites on bare Zn and UiO-67-3D@Zn electrodes after deposition. For comparison, the peak of (002) was much lower for UiO-67-2D@Zn electrode after deposition, which is in line with the inhibitory effects of UiO-67-2D nanoplates on dendrite formation. In addition, UiO-67-3D and UiO-67- 2D were stable after multiple-cycle deposition, as confirmed by the maintained MOF diffraction peaks in PXRD patterns of UiO-67-3D@Zn and UiO-67-2D@Zn (Figure S7).
Linear polarization curves were collected to study the protective effect of MOF coatings against Zn corrosion in the electrolyte. The corrosion currents of bare Zn, UiO-67-3D $@$ and UiO-67-2D@Zn were 12.1, 4.9 and $0.44\upmu\mathrm{Acm}^{-2}$ , respectively, with the corresponding voltages of $-0.036$ , $-0.023$ and $-0.021\mathrm{~V~}$ (Figure S8). The smaller corrosion current and positively shifted corrosion voltage indicate the anti-corrosion effect of MOF layers. In-situ optical microscope images were further adopted to monitor the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution on different electrode surfaces. $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ bubbles rapidly emerged on a bare $Z\mathrm{n}$ surface within $30\mathrm{min}$ , suggesting pronounced HER (Figure S9a). The UiO-67-2D and UiO-67- 3D coatings alleviated the HER, as indicated by the clean surface after plating for $60\mathrm{min}$ (Figure S9b, c). $E x$ -situ optical micrographs in Figure S10 further show the dendrite growth of bare $Z\mathrm{n}$ , UiO-67-3D@Zn, and UiO-67-2D $@z\mathbf{n}$ after being plated in symmetric cells for 30 and $60\mathrm{min}$ at $1\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . To quantitate $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production during plating/ stripping, we used in situ gas GC-MS to detect the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ flux of each electrode in 2 M $\mathrm{ZnSO_{4}}$ using symmetric cells $(3\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , $1.5\mathrm{\mAh\cm}^{-2}$ ). The $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ flux of bare $Z\mathrm{n}$ anodes in symmetric $Z\mathrm{n}//Z\mathrm{n}$ cell reaches $2.6\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{h}^{-1}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ during plating/stripping for $^{3\mathrm{{h}}}$ (Figure 5d), while the UiO-67- $30@Z{\bmod{n}}$ $\mathrm{1.1mmolh^{-1}c m^{-2}},$ ) and $\mathrm{UiO-}67{\mathrm{-}}2\mathrm{D}\textcircled{a}\mathrm{Zn}$ $(0.5\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{h}^{-1}\mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ exhibit significantly reduced $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ flux (Figure 5e, f). These results agree with the higher CE of UiO67-2D measured using symmetric cells at a current density of $8.0\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ and a capacity of $1.0\ \mathrm{mAh\cm}^{-2}$ (Figure S11). The protective effect of MOF layers can be explained by their ability to separate water from $Z\mathrm{n}$ surface, which significantly reduced $Z\mathrm{n}$ corrosion and enhanced the stability. The better performance of $\mathrm{UiO}{-}67{-}2\mathrm{D}@\mathrm{Zn}$ than UiO-67- $30@Z{\bmod{n}}$ can be explained by the dense packing of UiO-67- 2D nanosheets.
## 2.3 Mechanistic studies of Zn deposition
To mechanistically understand the effect of MOF coatings and explain the difference in protecting performances of UiO-67-3D and UiO-67-2D, DFT calculations were adopted.
Considering the morphologies of nanoparticles, we used the most exposed crystal facets (111 for UiO-67-3D octahedral nanoparticles and 001 for UiO-67-2D nanoplates) to build our DFT models (Figure S12). The adsorption energy of $Z\mathrm{n}$ atoms on different sites of $\mathrm{MOF/Zn}$ interface was calculated to locate the preferred binding sites of Zn. Potential binding sites evaluated in the DFT calculation include the BPDC ligands (Figure 6b) and the $\mathrm{\Zr-OH/H_{2}O}$ groups (Figure 6c). To compare the contribution of MOF coatings to $Z\mathrm{n}$ binding, the $Z\mathrm{n}$ adsorption energy on each site was referenced to the Zn adsorption energy on the bare $Z\mathrm{n}$ surface (Figure 6a). It was found that $Z\mathrm{n}$ preferentially bound to $\mathrm{\DeltaZr{-}O H/H_{2}O}$ ( $\Delta G=$ $-62.3\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}.$ ) over the BPDC ligand $\begin{array}{r l}{\Delta G}&{{}=}\end{array}$ $-37.1\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1},$ ) on the UiO-67-2D interface, which can be explained by the polar $\mathrm{\Zr{-}O H/H_{2}O}$ groups favoring $Z\mathrm{n}$ binding. A similar trend was also observed for the UiO-67- 3D interface (Table S1, Supporting Information online). Therefore, the $\mathrm{\Zr-OH/H_{2}O}$ groups on the $\mathrm{MOF/Zn}$ interface act as strong $Z\mathrm{n}$ binding sites where Zn deposition initiates. This computational result is in line with the reduced overpotential of $Z\mathrm{n}$ plating (i.e., lower nucleation barrier) by MOF coatings.
It should be noted that UiO-67-2D has a much higher $Z\mathrm{r}$ - $\mathrm{OH}/\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ concentration than UiO-67-3D, which was verified by DRIFTS, TGA, and contact angle measurements. The peaks at $3{,}670\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ can be assigned to the bridging $\upmu_{3}{\mathrm{-OH}}$ groups, while the broad band centered at $3{,}270\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ corresponds to the hydrogen-bonded $\mathrm{\Zr-OH/H_{2}O}$ (Figure 6d). UiO-67-2D shows a stronger adsorption band at $3{,}270\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ than UiO-67-3D, indicating more $\mathrm{\Zr{-}O H/H_{2}O}$ groups. The content of $\mathrm{\DeltaZr-OH/H_{2}O}$ was further quantified by TGA (Figure 6e). The weight losses at 300 and $550^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ are attributed to the removal of $\mathrm{\Zr-OH/H_{2}O}$ and the framework decomposition, respectively. The weight loss of UiO-67-2D at ${\sim}350^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ $(12.3~\mathrm{wt\%})$ is significantly higher than that of UiO-67-3D $(4.5~\mathrm{wt\%})$ , indicating a higher concentration of $\mathrm{\Zr{-}O H/H_{2}O}$ . Further evidence was provided by contact angle measurements. The water contact angle of UiO-67-2D $@Z{\bmod{n}}$ $(55.1^{\circ}$ , Figure 6h) is lower than that of Ui $\mathrm{\partial}){-}67{-}3\mathrm{D}\mathscr{Q}Z\mathrm{n}$ $(66.6^{\circ}$ , Figure $6\mathrm{g}$ ) and bare Zn $(87.9^{\circ}$ , Figure 6f). Coating MOFs onto the Zn surface increased the hydrophilicity due to the hydrogen bond interactions between $\mathrm{\Zr{-}O H/H_{2}O}$ groups and water molecules. The higher hydrophilicity of UiO-67-2D@Zn than $\mathrm{UiO}{-}67{-}3\mathrm{D}@Z\mathrm{n}$ indicates more $Z\mathrm{r}.$ - $\mathrm{OH}/\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in UiO-67-2D. The different ${\cal Z}\mathrm{r-OH/H_{2}O}$ concentrations in UiO-67-2D and UiO-67-3D originated from their structures and morphologies. UiO-67-3D is composed of coordinatively saturated $\mathrm{\small{[Zr_{6}(\mu_{3}-O)_{4}(\mu_{3}-O H)_{4}](C O_{2})_{12}}}$ clusters (Figure $6\mathrm{g}$ inset), whereas UiO-67-2D contains a $[Z\mathrm{r}_{12}(\upmu_{3}{-}\mathrm{O})_{8}(\upmu_{3}{-}\mathrm{OH})_{8}(\upmu_{2}{-}\mathrm{OH})_{6}](\mathrm{OH})_{6}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{6}(\mathrm{CO}_{2})_{12}$ cluster with six pairs of terminal $\mathrm{Zr-OH/H_{2}O}$ (Figure 6h inset). In addition, the nanosheet morphology of UiO-67-2D shows exposed $\mathrm{\Zr{-}O H/H_{2}O}$ on the (001) surface. Although terminal $\mathrm{\Zr-OH/H_{2}O}$ also exists on the (111) surface of UiO-67-3D nanoparticles, the ratio of surface atoms in octahedral nanoparticles is expected to be lower than that of nanosheets. In summary, the higher cycling stability of $\mathrm{JiO-}67{\cdot}2\mathrm{D}\mathscr{@}\mathrm{Zn}$ than Ui $\mathrm{)-}67-3\mathrm{D}\mathscr{Q}Z\mathrm{n}$ is attributed to the more exposed Zr$\mathrm{OH}/\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ as the zincophilic site, which induces uniform $Z\mathrm{n}$ deposition. Compared with octahedral nanoparticles of UiO67-3D, the oriented UiO-67-2D nanosheets also ensure closer contact with the Zn surface to inhibit Zn dendrites and side reactions. The vertically aligned MOF channels further regulate Zn diffusion, leading to dendrite-free Zn deposition. These results highlighted the unique advantage of 2D MOF nanosheets as protective coatings for Zn anodes.
## 2.4 Full cell performance
To further evaluate the performance of $\mathrm{MOF}@Z\mathrm{n}$ anodes in practical ZIBs, $\mathrm{Mn}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{C}$ nanoflakes derived from Mn-MOF were employed as the cathode materials to assemble $\mathrm{MOF@Zn\rVertMn_{2}O_{3}/C}$ cells. $\mathrm{{Mn}_{2}O_{3}/C}$ cathode was derived from Mn-MOF, and it displayed a nanoflake structure. The corresponding mapping shows that C, Mn, and O are uniformly distributed (Figure S13). Raman spectrum of $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Mn}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/$
C manifests the D band at $1{,}340\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , G band at $1{,}599\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , and $I_{\mathrm{G}}/I_{\mathrm{D}}$ of 1.43, indicating the graphitic carbon (Figure S14a). The spin-orbit splitting value from XPS of $\mathrm{Mn}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{C}$ is $11.3\mathrm{eV},$ , which matches that of $\mathrm{Mn}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ . The peaks of Mn $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ at $651.53\mathrm{eV}$ and Mn $2{\tt p}_{3/2}$ at $639.9\mathrm{eV}$ belong to $\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}$ . Mn $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ at $651.59\mathrm{eV}$ and $\mathrm{Mn}2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ at $641.0\mathrm{eV}$ are attributed to $\mathrm{Mn}^{3+}$ . $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Mn}}2\ensuremath{\mathrm{p}}_{1/2}$ at $653.05\mathrm{eV}$ and Mn $2{\tt p}_{3/2}$ at $642.4\mathrm{eV}$ are assigned to $\mathrm{Mn}^{4+}$ . Shake-up peaks are situated at $644.8\mathrm{eV}$ (Figure S14c). The $\mathrm{~o~}$ 1s peak at $529\mathrm{eV}$ is from surface lattice oxygen, that at $531.3\mathrm{eV}$ is due to adsorbed oxygen, and that at $533.0\mathrm{eV}$ was attributed to adsorbed water (Figure S14d). $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption and desorption curve and pore size distribution indicate a specific surface area of $265\mathrm{\m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and a pore size of approximately $10\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure S14e). PXRD patterns of Mn-MOF and $\mathrm{Mn}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{C}$ are consistent with simulations, confirming the phase purity (Figure S14f) [42]. The ZIBs were tested in electrolytes containing $2\mathrm{M}\mathrm{ZnSO}_{4}$ and $0.1\mathrm{~M~MnSO_{4}}$ (Figures S15, S16a). EIS shows that the bulk resistance of MOF-coated Zn full cell is larger than that of bare Zn, possibly due to the poor electrical conductivity of MOF coatings. However, the higher slope in the low-frequency region for MOF-coated full cells indicates faster Zn2+ diffusion, which is consistent with the high $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ conductivity of MOF coatings. The cyclic voltammetry (CV) curves of the ZIBs with different anodes (Figure S16b) display identical redox peaks, proving the negligible impact of MOF coating on $Z\mathfrak{n}^{\bar{2}+}$ diffusion in ZIBs [43]. The slightly smaller voltage polarization of $\mathrm{UiO{-}67{-}2D@Z n||M n_{2}O_{3}/C}$ cathode reveals its better reversibility than that of UiO- $-67-3\mathrm{D}\textcircled{a}Z\mathrm{n}$ and bare $Z\mathrm{n}$ , possibly due to the lower Zn nucleation barrier on the Zr$\mathrm{OH}/\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ sites. ZIBs with different anodes also display similar galvanostatic charge-discharge (GCD) curves, and $\mathrm{JiO-67-2D}@Z{\mathrm{n}}||\mathbf{M}{\mathrm{n}}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{3}/\mathbf{C}$ , UiO- $\mathrm{-67-3D(\overline{{\omega}}Z n||M n_{2}O_{3}/C,}$ and $\mathrm{Zn}||\mathrm{Mn}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{C}$ ZIBs achieve the capacities of 240, 212 and $210\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}$ at ${\mathrm{~1~A~g~}}^{-1,}$ respectively (Figure S16c). Rate capability tests show that the specific capacity of UiO-67- $2\mathrm{D}@\mathrm{Zn}||\mathrm{Mn}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{C}$ gradually decreases as the current density increases (Figure S17), but the capacity is fully recovered to $240\mathrm{\mAh\vec{\g}^{-1}}$ when the cycling rate is switched back to $\mathrm{~1~A~g~}^{-1}$ . However, the capacity of UiO- $\mathrm{\cdot67{-}3D\mathcal{Q}Z n||M n_{2}O_{3}/C}$ and $\mathrm{Zn}||\mathrm{Mn}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{C}$ ZIBs exhibits a quick decay with current densities increasing to $5\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ . When switched back to $\mathrm{~1~A~g~}^{-1}$ , they were unable to reach the initial level, indicating an inferior rate performance due to high charge transfer resistance (Figure S16d). More importantly, U $\mathrm{iO-}67{\cdot}2\mathrm{D}\mathcal{Q}Z\mathrm{n}||$ $\mathrm{Mn}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{C}$ shows high cycling stability with $81\%$ retention of initial capacitance and a CE of nearly $100\%$ after 1,500 cycles at $2\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ (Figure S16e), which is more stable than that of UiO-67-3D $@Z{\bmod{n}}$ ( $64\%$ capacitance retention) and bare Znbased ZIBs $42\%$ capacitance retention). To display the practical application of ZIBs, UiO- $S7{-}2\mathrm{D}@\mathrm{Zn}||\mathrm{Mn}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{C}$ lighted up 12 light-emitting diodes (LEDs) and a digital watch (Figures S16e inset).
## 3 Conclusions
In conclusion, UiO-67-2D and UiO-67-3D were applied as Zn anodes to investigate the impact of MOF structures and morphologies on the cycling performance of $Z\mathrm{n}$ anodes. Compared with UiO- $-67-3\mathrm{D}\textcircled{a}Z\mathrm{n}$ , the enhanced performance of the UiO-67-2D@Zn anode was attributed to the higher concentration of $\mathrm{\Zr-OH/H_{2}O}$ as zincophilic sites to induce uniform $Z\mathrm{n}$ deposition. The nanosheet morphology further ensured close contact with the $Z\mathrm{n}$ surface, which prevents HER reactions while permitting $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ diffusion through the perpendicularly aligned channels. The U $\mathrm{iO-}67{\mathrm{-}}2\mathrm{D}\mathscr{Q}\mathrm{Zn}||$ $\mathrm{Mn}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{C}$ cell achieves high capacity ( $240\ \mathrm{\mAh}\ \mathrm{\g}^{-1}$ at $1\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}\dot{}$ ), good rate performance and cycling stability $81\%$ capacity retention after 1,500 cycles at $2\mathrm{Ag}^{-1})$ . This unique Zn anode design using 2D MOF coatings not only provided a promising anode candidate for ZIBs, but also related the MOF structures/morphologies to the performance of protected $Z\mathrm{n}$ anodes. This mechanistic understanding of the structure-property relationship will guide the design of protective materials for $Z\mathrm{n}$ anodes. Future work will focus on the surface engineering of MOF nanosheets by polar functional groups to tune Zn deposition and further enhance Zn anode stability. Furthermore, considering the precisely controllable surface structures and pore environments of MOFs, surface coatings with high stability and $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ionic conductivity can be envisioned for advanced aqueous zinc ion batteries.
